      <title>Celia Paul: ‘Writing is a journey on the ground. Whereas if you paint you see e…</title>
      <description><![CDATA[<p><a href="https://archive.is/Vw72n#selection-1151.0-1159.65">Celia Paul: ‘Writing is a journey on the ground. Whereas if you paint you see everything at once’</a></p>
<blockquote>
<p>We are now outside the Pensione Calcina, where the art critic and writer John Ruskin took a room in 1876–1877 while working on <em>The Stones of Venice</em>. “It’s also where Annie Ernaux stayed when she wrote <em>Simple Passion</em>,” says Paul. Ernaux’s book charted an all-consuming love affair.</p>
</blockquote>
